# Memcheck suppressions for Arora (MEM01).
# Load with:  --suppressions=.devin/valgrind.supp
#
# Unlike LSan, valgrind suppressions match an ordered top-of-stack
# pattern, so these can be made tight.  Only upstream Qt/system noise
# lives here — a leak or invalid access allocated in Arora's own code
# must NEVER be suppressed; fix it instead.
#
# QT_ENABLE_REGEXP_JIT=0 is set by check-leaks.sh for this pass:
# pcre2's JIT emits self-modifying code that memcheck cannot model
# (thousands of Addr16/Cond false positives inside compiled regexes).

# Qt 6.11.3 application init: an SSE-16 compare inside
# QObject::connect reads up to 3 bytes past the end of a heap
# QByteArray during QGuiApplicationPrivate::init (upstream Qt issue,
# seen on every GUI test at startup — one context per binary).
# NOTE: fun: lines must use MANGLED symbols — this Qt build ships
# without .debug_frame for these paths, so memcheck matches raw
# symbol names (demangled patterns do not fire; verified 3.26.0).
{
   qt6-init-connect-addr16
   Memcheck:Addr16
   obj:*/libQt6Core.so*
   ...
   fun:_ZN7QObject7connectEPKS_PKcS1_S3_N2Qt14ConnectionTypeE
   fun:_ZN22QGuiApplicationPrivate4initEv
   ...
}
